---
/**
 * Long-form copy about the tool for the home page (~1,000 words), aimed at the
 * "AQI today / live AQI / AQI near me / air quality index" searches in
 * .docs/seo-keywords.md. The level ranges come from the same tables the dashboards use.
 */
import Icon from '../ui/Icon.astro';
import { STANDARDS, type StandardId } from '../../lib/aqi/standards';
import { POPULAR_CITIES } from '../../lib/seo';

const scales = (['in', 'us'] as const).map((id: StandardId) => ({
  id,
  title: id === 'in' ? 'India NAQI (CPCB)' : 'US AQI (EPA)',
  rows: STANDARDS[id].categories.map((c) => ({
    label: c.label,
    color: c.color,
    range: c.max === Infinity ? `${c.min}+` : `${c.min}–${c.max}`,
  })),
}));
---

<section class="shell py-20 lg:py-28" aria-labelledby="about-tool-title">
  <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.6fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
    <article class="prose-reading" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="about-tool-title" class="type-section mt-0">Live AQI: check today’s air quality near you</h2>
      <p>
        Live AQI is a free, real-time air quality index for every city in the world. Open the page and it shows the
        <strong>AQI today</strong> for where you are: one number from 0 to 500, its colour and level, the pollutant driving it
        and what it means for your health. There is no account to create and no app to install; the live air quality index
        works on any phone or computer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Air pollution changes by the hour. A morning that starts “Moderate” can turn “Poor” by evening as traffic builds, the
        wind drops or smoke drifts in from crop fires. Live AQI is built for the moment you need to decide whether to go for a
        run, open the windows or send the children out to play. It answers one question with numbers you can check: is the air
        safe right now, and what should I do about it?
      </p>

      <h3>What Live AQI shows for every place</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The current AQI on India’s National Air Quality Index (CPCB) and on the US EPA AQI, with its level from Good to Severe or Hazardous.</li>
        <li>Pollutant levels for PM2.5, PM10, ozone (O₃), nitrogen dioxide (NO₂), sulphur dioxide (SO₂) and carbon monoxide (CO), in µg/m³.</li>
        <li>The AQI over the last 24 hours and 7 days, plus a 48-hour air quality forecast.</li>
        <li>Health advice for the current level: who is at risk, whether to exercise outdoors, and when to wear a mask or close the windows.</li>
        <li>The weather that shapes pollution, such as temperature, humidity, wind and rain.</li>
        <li>Nearby places and, where available, ground monitoring stations.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>How to check the AQI near you</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>Open Live AQI. The dashboard starts from an approximate location based on your connection, or the place you chose last time.</li>
        <li>Tap “Use my location” for a reading for your exact area, or search any city, town or village. Coordinates are rounded to about 1 km.</li>
        <li>Read the AQI number and its colour, then the health advice beneath it.</li>
        <li>Check the 48-hour forecast to plan the cleanest time to be outdoors.</li>
        <li>Turn on <a href="/alerts">air quality alerts</a> to hear when the air crosses the level you choose.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>India AQI and US AQI on one page</h3>
      <p>
        India and the United States measure the same pollutants but grade them differently. India’s National Air Quality Index,
        published by the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), runs from Good (0–50) and Satisfactory (51–100) through
        Moderate, Poor and Very Poor to Severe (above 400), and averages particles over 24 hours. The US AQI uses tighter bands,
        where anything above 150 is already “Unhealthy”, and NowCast averages that react faster to change. Live AQI computes
        both from the same hourly data, shows the India NAQI by default for places in India and the US AQI everywhere else, and
        lets you switch with one tap. The two are never blended into one number. Learn <a href="/what-is-aqi">what each AQI level means</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>AQI today in Delhi, Mumbai and other cities</h3>
      <p>
        Interest in the AQI peaks every winter in North India, when Delhi, Noida, Gurgaon and Ghaziabad often reach “Very
        Poor” or “Severe” levels. Every city has its own page with the live AQI today, the highest reading of the past week,
        the forecast and answers to common questions. The most searched city pages:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 flex list-none flex-wrap gap-2 pl-0" aria-label="Popular city AQI pages">
        {
          POPULAR_CITIES.slice(0, 12).map((c) => (
            <li class="mt-0">
              <a href={`/city/${c.slug}`} class="chip no-underline hover:underline">
                {c.label} AQI
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Beyond India, Live AQI covers every country. Open the <a href="/map">live AQI map</a> to see air quality across the
        world, the <a href="/rankings">AQI ranking</a> for the most polluted and cleanest cities right now, or a country page
        such as <a href="/country/india">India AQI</a> to compare its cities and regions.
      </p>

      <h3>Where the data comes from</h3>
      <p>
        Live AQI calculates every index itself from hourly pollutant concentrations produced by the Copernicus Atmosphere
        Monitoring Service (CAMS), the European Union’s atmosphere monitoring programme, and delivered by Open-Meteo. CAMS
        combines satellite observations, ground measurements and weather models into a consistent picture of the atmosphere,
        at about 11 km resolution over Europe and 45 km worldwide. That is why Live AQI can show a reading, a week of history
        and a forecast for any village, not only for cities with a monitoring station. Because this is model data, a single
        roadside monitor can read higher or lower. Every reading is labelled with its source and time, and if a provider is
        down, Live AQI says so instead of guessing. The <a href="/methodology">methodology</a> lists every breakpoint and rule.
      </p>

      <h3>Pollution alerts that respect you</h3>
      <p>
        Choose a place and a level on the alerts page, and Live AQI tells you when the air there crosses it: while the page is
        open, or as a background notification on your phone or computer. It can also tell you when the air recovers, and warn
        you about severe pollution. There are no accounts and no sale of personal data; the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>{' '}
        explains exactly what is stored.
      </p>

      <h3>Who Live AQI is for</h3>
      <p>
        Parents deciding whether it is a day for the park, runners and cyclists planning a route, people with asthma or COPD
        watching for bad-air days, and anyone who wants to know what they are breathing. It also helps schools, offices and
        housing societies time outdoor activities around the cleanest hours. Live AQI provides information, not medical advice:
        in an emergency, follow your local health authority.
      </p>
    </article>

    <aside class="lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start" aria-label="AQI levels at a glance" data-reveal>
      <div class="card p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="font-bold">AQI levels at a glance</p>
        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-1 xl:grid-cols-2">
          {
            scales.map((s) => (
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink-3">{s.title}</p>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1.5 text-sm">
                  {s.rows.map((r) => (
                    <li class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                      <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
                        <span class="swatch" style={`--cat:${r.color}`} />
                        {r.label}
                      </span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap text-ink-2">{r.range}</span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2 border-t border-line pt-5">
          <a href="/what-is-aqi" class="btn btn-secondary">AQI meaning <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a>
          <a href="/alerts" class="btn btn-primary"><Icon name="bell" size={17} /> Get alerts</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>
